---
name: feature
description: Golden path from idea to merged, verified change across the hub's repos. Chooses the ceremony by process size (spike, bounded, architectural; a spike runs the spike workflow) and chains research, plan, TDD implementation and review.
disable-model-invocation: true
argument-hint: "<idea or tracker issue id>"
---

# /feature <idea>

Paths below are relative to the hub (the directory with `hub.json`); repos are its siblings, listed in `hub.json` → `repos`, each with its own `AGENTS.md`.
Agents are the hub's project subagents (`subagent_type: <name>`, from `.claude/agents/`; `hub-workflow:<name>` when installed as a plugin); each reads the brain first and ends with a compound step (`/learn` proposal or "no learning").

**Minimum-sufficient rule** (every step and agent below): build the smallest change that solves today's problem. A generic or shared module needs a named second consumer that exists now. Add constants, arguments, options and tests together with their first real user. Prefer not doing something (e.g. not logging a payload) over building machinery to do it safely.

**A ticket of a planned feature** (an id listed as a ticket's `id` in a `brain/features/*/features.json`): do not restart brief, sizing or spec; point to `/implement <id>` and stop.

0. **Brief** (every size, spikes included): the work starts from a brief in its tracker issue's description, one Markdown heading per section: @@{brief_sections}. A heading is a run of `#` to `######` then a space and the name (`#Problem` and a closing `##` don't match); it matches ignoring case, its level, surrounding spaces and a trailing `:`; use these exact names (no aliases); headings inside code blocks don't count. A section counts when its body, up to the next heading of the same or a higher level, holds more than a bare placeholder (@@{brief_placeholders}); order and extra sections are free. Given an issue id: read its description (if it cannot be read, say so and stop) and check it. Given only an idea, or with sections missing: draft each missing section with the user, one question at a time, then write them into the tracker issue (if there is none, create it in @@{tracker_team_key}; in an existing issue append only the missing sections, show the change and ask before writing). The Outcome holds the line "How we will know after merge that it solved the problem" with a signal anyone can check, not a restatement of the ACs: a command that exits 0 from a repo root on the default branch, within the `post-merge-verify` pack's limits (no session, no absolute or worktree path, no `origin/...` range), recorded as an AC's verification so the pack runs it (or as the issue's Verification command when it has no ACs); a `/retro` number past a value by a date; or no new bug of a given kind for N weeks. If the line is missing or holds no such signal, ask for one (one question) before sizing, then add it to the Outcome section (show the change and ask before writing). Then size it. `./hub run` refuses an issue whose brief is incomplete.
1. **Size it** (ask if unclear): *spike* (throwaway learning, no merge), *bounded* (the default: ≤ a few PRs, known design), *architectural* (a new contract between repos or services, or a decision that warrants an ADR). A path under `guard.ask_before_edit` alone does not make it architectural: it adds the guard's ask and the reviewers' check. A bug or a small bounded task within `/fix`'s limits (one repo, at most 5 changed non-test files, no new contract, no path under `guard.ask_before_edit`) goes to `/fix` instead (create its tracker issue first if there is none).
   Other work has its own workflow: a bug with no reproduction goes to `/incident`, a behaviour-preserving refactor to `/refactor`, a dependency or toolchain bump to `/upgrade`, a standalone decision to `/adr`. If `AGENTS.project.md` is empty (no project rules yet) or `hub.json` → `repos` has a new repo, one whose `dir` `AGENTS.project.md` does not name, suggest `/onboard` before anything else.
2. **Spec** (bounded/architectural), **here in the main session** (subagents can't ask the user): one question at a time, then 2–3 approaches with a recommendation; if superpowers is enabled, use its `brainstorming`, otherwise lead it yourself. Then the **`requirements-analyst`** agent writes
   `brain/features/<slug>/spec.md` (AC ids, Given/When/Then, invariants, repos). Each time `spec.md`, `research.md` or `plan.md` is written or amended, run `/summarize <slug> <record>` (hub web shows people that summary). **Gate: user approves the spec** (architectural; bounded may merge spec+plan approval).
   A spec with `status: approved` is never rewritten: a later change goes to its `## Amendments` section (approved like the spec), a new AC takes the next free id, a dropped one stays listed as withdrawn (`withdrawn: true` in `features.json`), and AC ids are never renumbered.
3. **Research**: **`researcher`** agent (or `/research` inline for small questions). **Plan**: **`architect`** agent → `plan.md`; each architectural decision it needs is recorded with `/adr` (its steps 1–5 on this issue; in-line on the feature's worktree, and on the Build path its step 6 too) → **gate: user approves the plan**.
4. **Tickets**: **`planner`** agent → `features.json` (its `tickets` plan with `blocked_by`) + `issues.md`; from here, create the issues in the tracker (@@{tracker_team_key}) in plan order, fill each ticket's `id` in `features.json` as its issue is created, then write their ids into `brain/features/<slug>/features.json` → `linear` (parent first, then children in contract order), create the tracker's "blocked by" relations from each ticket's `blocked_by`, and run `./hub doctor --only features.tracker` (it validates the record and its ticket plan, and warns while neither `linear` nor any ticket's `id` holds an id). `features.json` is the source of truth for the ids (`hub web` reads them there); spec.md `linear:` stays optional.
5. **Build** (bounded/architectural, once **Tickets** is done): follow `/implement`'s steps once per ticket the planning lists as can-start (every ticket in its `blocked_by` done, not started yet: hub web's Tickets tab, "Can start now"), one ticket at a time; each run does its ticket's context, preflight, worktree, ticket plan, review rounds, ticket eval and PR, so steps 6 and 7 do not run here for a ticket. Go on as tickets merge and others can start; a run paused on a plan change request waits for the user. Step 8 then evaluates the whole feature.
6. **Implement** (a spike, or work no `/implement` run builds): the workspace first, `./hub worktree <team>-<n>-<slug> [--only <repo>]` (`make worktree NAME=…`) (named by the tracker issue) (isolated worktrees from a freshly fetched default branch); then one task per fresh subagent, building only what the task needs (the minimum-sufficient rule above), failing test first, the repo's `check_fast` (from `hub.json`) green after each task: if superpowers is enabled, use its `subagent-driven-development` + `test-driven-development`; otherwise dispatch each task yourself with its plan text and verification command. Each implementer changes files with the Edit or Write tool, never by a shell script, so the session's review shows each change.
   Never weaken existing assertions.
7. **Review each task twice** (fresh contexts): **`spec-reviewer`** (matches the plan, nothing missing, nothing extra), then **`quality-reviewer`**
   (domain invariants, security, idempotency, what is extra), passing each the `ticket`, `task`, `round` (from 1) and `commit` it reviews.
   Save each reply's fenced `json` block verbatim to `brain/features/<slug>/reviews/<ticket>/<reviewer>-r<N>.json` (`<reviewer>` `spec` or `quality`, `N` the ticket's review round, counted across all its tasks and never restarted per task, so no file is overwritten) and run `./hub validate-output review <file>`.
   A reply with no fenced `json` block, or more than one, counts as invalid: re-ask that reviewer once, as for an invalid file.
   When it exits non-zero, re-ask that reviewer once, passing every line the command printed (the `… and N more` line included), and save the new reply to the same file; when the second reply is invalid too, stop and report to the user: no repair, no guessing.
   Act only on validated files: send `blocking` findings back to the implementer; weigh each `minor` one: send it back when it names a concrete current failure mode, otherwise reject it with a one-line reason. A re-review after fixes is the next round.
   After a ticket's reviews, write `brain/features/<slug>/reviews/<ticket>.md` (`<ticket>` its tracker id) with frontmatter `type: review`, `ticket: <id>`, `outcome: approved | changes-requested`, `pr: <repo>#<n>`, `reviewed_at: <date>` and the reviewers' findings: `changes-requested` while fixes are pending, `approved` once both reviewers pass: the latest round of each is a validated `PASS`. Only `approved` counts the ticket as reviewed.
8. **Verify**: **`evaluator`** agent, given the scope (a ticket, a PR or the whole feature) → a new `brain/features/<slug>/evals/<delivery>.md` (never overwrites one) + `features.json` flipped with evidence; then act on its validated verdict (below).
   Run `./hub validate-output eval <file>` on the evaluator's `brain/features/<slug>/evals/<delivery>.json`. When it is missing or the command exits non-zero, re-dispatch the evaluator once with the same scope, the failed delivery and every line the command printed (the `… and N more` line included): it writes a new `<delivery>-rN` pair and the failed pair stays as evidence; validate that new `-rN` JSON. A second missing or invalid output stops: report it to the user, no ship.
   Act only on the validated JSON: ship on a validated `SHIP`, or on a validated `SHIP-WITH-OWNER-STEPS` once the user confirms its owner steps are done; on `SHIP-WITH-OWNER-STEPS` list its `owner_steps` to the user and stop until they confirm, with no new evaluation; on `NO-SHIP` send its failing `results` and `gates` back to the implementer and, after the fix, run a fresh evaluation (a new `<delivery>-rN` pair). On the Build path, a `NO-SHIP` goes to a new `/implement` run of each ticket its failing `results` name, whose own PR carries the fix, then to a fresh evaluation. The re-dispatch-once budget covers only a missing or invalid eval JSON, never a `NO-SHIP`.
   **Finish** (the in-line path of step 6 only; on the Build path each `/implement` run opened its ticket's PR): if superpowers is enabled, use its `finishing-a-development-branch`; otherwise push the branch and open the PR. Either way the PR is authored by the user on a `project.branch_prefix` branch: no AI co-author trailer, no "Generated with", no 🤖. The PR body is text for people: write it with the `plain-language` skill.
9. **Compound**: triage the agents' `brain/_inbox/` proposals with the user; `/handoff` if the work spans sessions.

*Spike*: step 0 still applies (a spike's acceptance criteria may be the questions it answers); skip 2, 4 and the plan gate; timebox; `researcher` findings in `brain/features/<slug>/research.md`; no merge.

*Spike run*: write the question with the Write tool to one absolute file path (resolve it first with `echo "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}"`, replacing a file left by an earlier run) and check it with `test -s <file>`. Then, from the hub, run `./hub workflow run spike --input question="$(cat "<file>")" --input slug=<slug> --input timebox=2h --live` (output redirected to a file, `$?` checked). The run does recall, research, 2–3 options, the user's pick, and a premortem of the pick. At the pick the run stops with `run <run>: paused at <gate>`: show the user the `message` of the last `"event": "gate"` line of `.agent-runs/workflows/<run>/records.jsonl` (the options and the recommendation), then pass on their answer with `./hub workflow respond <run> --choice a|b|c|more --text "<note>"` (never pick for them; `more` with a note asks for another round of options). It then writes `brain/features/<slug>/research.md` and an ADR draft beside it for `/adr`; it commits nothing (the user commits them with the feature's records). Its agents only read: do not edit the hub or a repo while it runs, since a change fails the step that is running. A run that reaches the timebox ends done with what it has, marked incomplete (no pick is asked for after it). If `./hub workflow run` is not available, follow the *Spike* line by hand.
